Add a --tsv flag to the csv2json CLI so it reads tab-separated input. Make one pull request that meets every acceptance line below.

Current state: bin/csv2json.js reads stdin and calls parse(text) with no separator, and it ignores argv. src/parse.js exports parse(text, sep = ','). test/csv.test.js uses node:test and already has a 'cli' test that runs bin/csv2json.js with execFileSync. package.json has "test": "node --test". README.md documents only CSV usage.

Steps:
1. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether '--tsv' appears anywhere in process.argv.slice(2). If it does, call parse(text, '\t'). If it does not, call parse(text) exactly as today. Leave the output format as it is: JSON.stringify of the array, then a single '\n'.
2. Do not change how fields are split (no quoting rules), do not add auto-detection, --sep/--delimiter or --help, and do not change how unknown arguments are handled. Leave src/parse.js alone unless you really have to change it, and if you do, the CSV behaviour must stay the same.
3. In test/csv.test.js (or a new test/*.test.js file), add node:test tests that run bin/csv2json.js with the argument '--tsv' through execFileSync on input with tabs and assert the exact stdout. Cover: 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' -> '[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]\n'; 'name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n' -> '[{"name":"x","note":"hello, world"}]\n'; and 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' -> '[{"a":"1","b":"2"},{"a":"3","b":"4"}]\n'. Also add a test that tab input without the flag still gives '[{"a\\tb":"1\\t2"}]\n' (a JSON tab escape inside the key and value). Keep the existing tests.
4. In README.md, add a section for the --tsv flag. Say that it reads tab-separated input and give the example invocation `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv`. Keep the existing CSV example.
5. From the repo root, run `npm test` and check that it exits 0. Run each acceptance command below by hand and check that the output is byte-exact.

Acceptance lines (all must hold):
- `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]` followed by a newline and exits 0.
- With --tsv, commas inside a field stay literal: `printf 'name\tnote\nx\thello, world\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{"name":"x","note":"hello, world"}]` followed by a newline.
- With --tsv and several data rows, every row comes out in order: `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n3\t4\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{"a":"1","b":"2"},{"a":"3","b":"4"}]` followed by a newline.
- Without the flag, CSV still works as before: `printf 'a,b\n1,2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{"a":"1","b":"2"}]` followed by a newline.
- Without the flag, tabs are not treated as separators: `printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{"a\tb":"1\t2"}]` followed by a newline, which is the current output.
- `npm test` exits 0, and test/ contains at least one test that runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and checks its output.
- README.md documents the --tsv flag with an example invocation.